low/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/jeasmith/ithilien/commit/db2a6bbcb38c836ec63bda1d64dbe49ff7e2318d","node_id":"C_kwDORgzHydoAKGRiMmE2YmJjYjM4YzgzNmVjNjNiZGExZDY0ZGJlNDlmZjdlMjMxOGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/jeasmith/ithilien/commit/c9d5926368cec6ca01345091bb41e6580a0f5036","sha":"c9d5926368cec6ca01345091bb41e6580a0f5036","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jeasmith/ithilien/commits/c9d5926368cec6ca01345091bb41e6580a0f5036"}],"sha":"db2a6bbcb38c836ec63bda1d64dbe49ff7e2318d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jeasmith/ithilien/commits/db2a6bbcb38c836ec63bda1d64dbe49ff7e2318d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6818323?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jeasmith","id":6818323,"login":"jeasmith","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4MTgzMjM=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jeasmith","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jeasmith/ithilien/commits/c9d5926368cec6ca01345091bb41e6580a0f5036/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T13:12:41Z","email":"jeasmith@me.com","name":"Jamie Smith"},"comment_count":1,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T13:12:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Use TypeScript 6 with Oxlint and project-local LSP (#78)\n\n* Add oxlint alongside ESLint\n\ntypescript-eslint pins the typescript peer to <6.1.0 and crashes on load\nagainst TypeScript 7, which blocks the compiler upgrade. Oxlint is written\nin Rust and never links the TypeScript compiler, so it is unaffected; its\ntype-aware pass runs through oxlint-tsgolint, which bundles its own\ntypescript-go and works against the current TypeScript 6.0.3.\n\nOxlint runs first, then ESLint. eslint-plugin-oxlint derives the overlap\nfrom .oxlintrc.json and switches off the 228 ESLint rules oxlint now\ncovers, so the two stay in sync without a hand-maintained list.\n\nThis also adds type-aware rules the project never had —\neslint-config-next/typescript is not type-aware, so no-floating-promises\nwas never running. It caught an incorrect await in eslint.config.mjs on\nthe first run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Upgrade to TypeScript 7, drop ESLint\n\nTwo independent blockers stood in the way of TypeScript 7, both from tools\nreaching for the compiler API rather than anything in our own code.\n\ntypescript-eslint links the compiler API directly and pins its typescript\npeer to <6.1.0; no published version supports 7, including canary. Pinning\nTypeScript 6 for the linter alone does not work, because typescript is a\npeer resolved from the root importer and pnpm overrides do not redirect\npeer resolution.\n\nAfter oxlint''s deduplication, ESLint contributed only 33 active rules, and\nonly the 14 React Compiler rules from eslint-plugin-react-hooks had no\noxlint equivalent. oxlint''s jsPlugins mechanism runs that plugin directly,\nparsing TSX with oxlint''s own Rust parser, so ESLint bought nothing.\neslint-plugin-react-hooks is kept and loaded by oxlint, aliased to\nreact-compiler to avoid colliding with the native react plugin.\n\nThe 14 rules oxlint supported but had not enabled are now listed\nexplicitly. Four are lost: react/no-deprecated and require-render-return\n(class components, none here) and jsx-uses-react and jsx-uses-vars (only\nmark vars used for no-unused-vars, which oxlint handles for JSX natively).\n\nNext.js was the second blocker — its build-time check uses the same\ncompiler API. experimental.useTypeScriptCli makes it shell out to tsc.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Address PR review feedback\n\nEnable type-aware linting via options.typeAware in .oxlintrc.json rather\nthan the --type-aware CLI flag, so editors and bare oxlint runs get the\nsame 295 rules as CI. Verified: a floating promise is now caught without\nany flag.\n\nRaise engines.node to ^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0 to match oxlint''s own\nrequirement. Node 20.0-20.18 and Node 21 satisfied the old >=20 range but\ncannot run oxlint''s native bindings.\n\nFix ADR-0012''s React rule count — 16 rules are enabled in total\n(rules-of-hooks, exhaustive-deps, and 14 React Compiler diagnostics), not\n16 compiler rules plus the two core ones. Add a language to the\nstack-trace fence in ADR-0011 for markdownlint MD040.\n\nNot applied: Codex''s suggestion to add --js-plugins to the lint scripts.\nThat flag does not exist in oxlint 1.75.0 (absent from --help), and\nconfig-declared jsPlugins run without it — verified by planting hook\nviolations and confirming pnpm lint fails on react-compiler(rules-of-hooks)\nand react-compiler(set-state-in-render).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin Node 24 LTS and add Docker-based local development\n\nLocal development required a matching Node.js on the host, and the pinned\nversions had drifted: .nvmrc said 20 while the toolchain floor was already\n^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0 for oxlint''s native bindings.\n\nStandardise on Node 24 LTS rather than the newest release. Node 26.5.0 is\nCurrent, but Vercel offers 24.x, 22.x and 20.x only, so 26 would guarantee\nlocal development ran a different major than production. Node 25 is odd-\nnumbered and already end-of-life. .nvmrc drives CI, engines.node is 24.x in\nthe form Vercel documents, and the Dockerfile pins the same major.\n\nAdd a dev target on node:24-bookworm-slim with compose.yaml for the\nday-to-day workflow and a devcontainer that reuses the same service. Debian\nover Alpine because glibc bindings for oxlint and Turbopack are the better\ntested path.\n\nnode_modules and .next are masked with named volumes rather than shared\nwith the host. oxlint and oxlint-tsgolint resolve platform-native binaries,\nso one shared tree would put darwin-arm64 builds on the path inside a Linux\ncontainer. The lockfile carries every platform''s bindings, so\n--frozen-lockfile resolves the linux set in the image.\n\nNot verified: no container runtime is available in this environment, so the\nimage has never been built or run. compose.yaml and devcontainer.json parse\nand the lockfile was confirmed to contain linux-arm64-gnu and linux-x64-gnu\nbindings, but the build itself is unexercised.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Use TypeScript 6 with Oxlint and project-local LSP\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8d317b7637c225970f1ee2743ff66cc89a25220a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jeasmith/ithilien/git/trees/8d317b7637c225970f1ee2743ff66cc89a25220a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jeasmith/ithilien/git/commits/c9d5926368cec6ca01345091bb41e6580a0f5036","verification":{"payload":"tree 8d317b7637c225970f1ee2743ff66cc89a25220a\nparent 813e5e61d10254f355b7e2afd49c1b15f9ee482e\nauthor Jamie Smith <jeasmith@me.com> 1788009161 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788009161 +0100\n\nUse TypeScript 6 with Oxlint and project-local LSP (#78)\n\n* Add oxlint alongside ESLint\n\ntypescript-eslint pins the typescript peer to <6.1.0 and crashes on load\nagainst TypeScript 7, which blocks the compiler upgrade. Oxlint is written\nin Rust and never links the TypeScript compiler, so it is unaffected; its\ntype-aware pass runs through oxlint-tsgolint, which bundles its own\ntypescript-go and works against the current TypeScript 6.0.3.\n\nOxlint runs first, then ESLint. eslint-plugin-oxlint derives the overlap\nfrom .oxlintrc.json and switches off the 228 ESLint rules oxlint now\ncovers, so the two stay in sync without a hand-maintained list.\n\nThis also adds type-aware rules the project never had —\neslint-config-next/typescript is not type-aware, so no-floating-promises\nwas never running. It caught an incorrect await in eslint.config.mjs on\nthe first run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Upgrade to TypeScript 7, drop ESLint\n\nTwo independent blockers stood in the way of TypeScript 7, both from tools\nreaching for the compiler API rather than anything in our own code.\n\ntypescript-eslint links the compiler API directly and pins its typescript\npeer to <6.1.0; no published version supports 7, including canary. Pinning\nTypeScript 6 for the linter alone does not work, because typescript is a\npeer resolved from the root importer and pnpm overrides do not redirect\npeer resolution.\n\nAfter oxlint''s deduplication, ESLint contributed only 33 active rules, and\nonly the 14 React Compiler rules from eslint-plugin-react-hooks had no\noxlint equivalent. oxlint''s jsPlugins mechanism runs that plugin directly,\nparsing TSX with oxlint''s own Rust parser, so ESLint bought nothing.\neslint-plugin-react-hooks is kept and loaded by oxlint, aliased to\nreact-compiler to avoid colliding with the native react plugin.\n\nThe 14 rules oxlint supported but had not enabled are now listed\nexplicitly. Four are lost: react/no-deprecated and require-render-return\n(class components, none here) and jsx-uses-react and jsx-uses-vars (only\nmark vars used for no-unused-vars, which oxlint handles for JSX natively).\n\nNext.js was the second blocker — its build-time check uses the same\ncompiler API. experimental.useTypeScriptCli makes it shell out to tsc.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Address PR review feedback\n\nEnable type-aware linting via options.typeAware in .oxlintrc.json rather\nthan the --type-aware CLI flag, so editors and bare oxlint runs get the\nsame 295 rules as CI. Verified: a floating promise is now caught without\nany flag.\n\nRaise engines.node to ^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0 to match oxlint''s own\nrequirement. Node 20.0-20.18 and Node 21 satisfied the old >=20 range but\ncannot run oxlint''s native bindings.\n\nFix ADR-0012''s React rule count — 16 rules are enabled in total\n(rules-of-hooks, exhaustive-deps, and 14 React Compiler diagnostics), not\n16 compiler rules plus the two core ones. Add a language to the\nstack-trace fence in ADR-0011 for markdownlint MD040.\n\nNot applied: Codex''s suggestion to add --js-plugins to the lint scripts.\nThat flag does not exist in oxlint 1.75.0 (absent from --help), and\nconfig-declared jsPlugins run without it — verified by planting hook\nviolations and confirming pnpm lint fails on react-compiler(rules-of-hooks)\nand react-compiler(set-state-in-render).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin Node 24 LTS and add Docker-based local development\n\nLocal development required a matching Node.js on the host, and the pinned\nversions had drifted: .nvmrc said 20 while the toolchain floor was already\n^20.19.0 || >=22.12.0 for oxlint''s native bindings.\n\nStandardise on Node 24 LTS rather than the newest release. Node 26.5.0 is\nCurrent, but Vercel offers 24.x, 22.x and 20.x only, so 26 would guarantee\nlocal development ran a different major than production. Node 25 is odd-\nnumbered and already end-of-life. .nvmrc drives CI, engines.node is 24.x in\nthe form Vercel documents, and the Dockerfile pins the same major.\n\nAdd a dev target on node:24-bookworm-slim with compose.yaml for the\nday-to-day workflow and a devcontainer that reuses the same service. Debian\nover Alpine because glibc bindings for oxlint and Turbopack are the better\ntested path.\n\nnode_modules and .next are masked with named volumes rather than shared\nwith the host. oxlint and oxlint-tsgolint resolve platform-native binaries,\nso one shared tree would put darwin-arm64 builds on the path inside a Linux\ncontainer. The lockfile carries every platform''s bindings, so\n--frozen-lockfile resolves the linux set in the image.\n\nNot verified: no container runtime is available in this environment, so the\nimage has never been built or run. compose.yaml and devcontainer.json parse\nand the lockfile was confirmed to contain linux-arm64-gnu and linux-x64-gnu\nbindings, but the build itself is unexercised.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Use TypeScript 6 with Oxlint and project-local LSP\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqktrJCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8tcQAHDBNkMWtnh2yazh8OzClMmC\nlPvM6Y+reIxbsRPhL5RnlaNLd+kT00dwxP0YjuWUeHEu/ysz9M1xN5g6HirLPX8W\n09cFHtwor0/w1NtXa1yeg6GsdgId0hLIZJtzHgxRNV/Q7u6MWcBDYqVljiIyKDDR\nlSvpewI8/na8114fYaa8syLrZgyLVt/GgoqxP9t0XZStUReJ6HGgqw3Bv0nBnp2v\nJx+6eGEAoYc36kCrY7DekV0cA8rEv5VaqBjNijW00VjVuMheKh9tZLxGxMNEUMG8\nVHAMCMPk4owyi03nohq1nCe5Rw7rVRTjY1G3UnAmx8h8sRzqekvudbNGFOXCWZag\nQpMh9